WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.980 line:1
Good to be with you.

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:01.900 line:1
I'm Tony Dokoupil.

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:06.240 line:1
The breaking news right now.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.500 line:1
Banning the press.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.860 line:1
I don't have to let them into the people's house.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.540 line:1
TONY DOKOUPIL: THE president kicking three news organizations out of the White House.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.900 line:1
What they're saying tonight.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.120 line:1
And can he do this?

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.180 line:1
Air emergency.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:24.540 line:1
The burned out cabin where a battery pack burst into flames hours into an overseas flight.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:30.000 line:1
What to know about the danger as more passengers bring devices onto planes.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.200 line:1
CBS News exclusive Patrick Clancy and what he wants the world to know following the killing of his three children.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.560 line:1
I live with the outcome.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:39.670 line:1
I live with it every day.

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:48.520 line:1
TONY DOKOUPIL: Our conversation with the 60 Minutes correspondent who got the interview as the lone holdout juror in Lindsay Clancy's murder trial breaks his silence.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.640 line:1
What he is now saying about his vote.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:57.540 line:1
Plus, the first interview with Princess Diana's brother after his accusations against the royal family.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.190 line:1
What he said about King Charles and Prince Harry.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:05.010 line:1
A mountain lion on the loose roaming the streets of San Francisco.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.750 line:1
What happened next?

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:07.550 line:1
Hero dog.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:13.390 line:1
The celebration for a police canine recovering after an on the job injury.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:20.590 line:1
And tonight, Steve Hartman shows us when you're a parent, the days may be long, but the years are short.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.470 line:1
STEVE HARTMAN: Why is it so hard then?

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:28.590 line:1
You reduce in size in their rear view mirror, but they stay the same for you.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.990 line:1
ANNOUNCER: From CBS News headquarters in New York.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:37.310 line:1
This is the CBS Evening News with Tony Dokoupil.

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:38.130 line:1
And good evening.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:45.890 line:1
President Trump today claimed he is banning a trio of major media companies from the White House, continuing a long, hostile relationship with the press.

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:58.690 line:1
But while he claimed on social media that the ban on CNN, MSNOW, and Politico was effective immediately, there was no immediate follow through aside from a new round of the same old name calling inside the White House.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:02.990 line:1
And as with past attempts to block coverage, he may lose this one in court.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:06.630 line:1
We get the latest now from our Weijia Jiang at the White House.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:14.450 line:1
WEIJIA JIANG: Tonight President Trump is defending his decision to ban CNN, Politico, and MSNOW from the White House.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.290 line:1
A very simple ban.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.110 line:1
I don't want them in my office.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:19.290 line:1
I don't want them here.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.290 line:1
I would say the ban would go as far as you can.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:32.970 line:1
WEIJIA JIANG: Trump first made the announcement on social media, claiming the outlet shouldn't be able to constantly write or report fiction and lies when they are covering the President of the United States.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:43.130 line:1
They purposely write negative news, and they do that because they want to try and diminish the Republicans in a Republican administration.

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:45.390 line:1
And honestly, we've done a fantastic job.

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:48.190 line:1
WEIJIA JIANG: Trump has a history of attacking the press.

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:50.810 line:1
Fake news doesn't report those stories.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.590 line:1
Boy, that's a lot of fake news.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.310 line:1
WEIJIA JIANG: And female reporters in particular.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:57.180 line:1
You're a loud, boisterous person.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:57.940 line:1
You're fake news.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:02:59.880 line:1
Be quiet.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:00.500 line:1
That's enough.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:01.340 line:1
Put down the mic.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.160 line:1
WEIJIA JIANG: He's also tried to restrict the media before.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:10.180 line:1
In his first term, he revoked Jim Acosta's White House credential and barred Kaitlan Collins from an event.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.560 line:1
Both were reporters for CNN.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:22.240 line:1
Last year, he kicked the Associated Press out of the Oval Office events because the outlet refused to use Trump's chosen name, "the Gulf of America."

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.640 line:1
This ban will likely be challenged in court.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:37.160 line:1
I think every time the president explains why he thinks this ban is necessary, it's actually worse for his legal case because he keeps talking about negative coverage and unfair coverage.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:42.680 line:1
And again, that's what the First Amendment protects against.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:48.660 line:1
In fact, in all those previous cases, access was restored to members of the press after legal battles.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:55.080 line:1
This evening, reporters from all three of the outlets Trump just threatened are here at the White House doing their jobs.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:57.640 line:1
And tell us they have not been asked to leave.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:58.140 line:1
Tony.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.460 line:1
Weijia, you know this train well.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.520 line:1
You were president of the White House Correspondents' Association.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.040 line:1
You understand the relationship between this White House and the press corps.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.850 line:1
What do you make of it all?

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:09.480 line:1
That's right.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.720 line:1
Tony, the Association is used to plenty of tension as we advocate for access.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:21.140 line:1
But while Trump has gone after reporters before, this is different because he may ban entire outlets from White House grounds.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:25.840 line:1
That's an escalation from keeping folks out of the Oval Office or off of Air Force One.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:37.240 line:1
Tonight, the WHCA says in a statement that the Constitution protects the freedom of the press from government interference, and that is ultimately for the good of the American public, Tony.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:38.980 line:1
Weijia, thank you very much.

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:45.400 line:1
Also today, President Trump announced a new deal with Greenland after threatening to take the island, citing US security concerns.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:52.570 line:1
The president said the agreement granted the US "permanent control over Greenland's security and all other needs."

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:57.890 line:1
Denmark, which handles the island's foreign policy, said the deal will be signed next week.

00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:08.870 line:1
A battery pack burst into flames yesterday hours into a flight from Amsterdam to Curacao, forcing the pilot, who was already out there over the ocean, to turn back toward land.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:11.350 line:1
Tonight, we're getting our first look at the damage.

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:16.760 line:1
CBS's Tom Hanson joins me now with the pictures and a warning for flyers.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:17.510 line:1
Hey there, Tony.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:18.340 line:1
Good evening to you.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:24.950 line:1
Well, some scary moments on a flight from Amsterdam yesterday after a power bank caught fire as the plane was over the Atlantic Ocean.

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:26.990 line:1
Just take a look at some of these images.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:31.450 line:1
The incident charred the window shade and injured one passenger who suffered minor burns.

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:35.430 line:1
The flight was headed to Curacao, but, of course, diverted back to Amsterdam.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:39.910 line:1
Now, lithium battery fires are a known issue and have been on the rise since 2020.

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:54.220 line:1
The FAA says there have been 77 incidents this year, 40 from battery packs, and just last month, firefighters had to rush on board an American flight in Dallas after reports of several passengers being burned when a laptop caught fire.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:06:02.900 line:1
Now, earlier this year, the FAA showed CBS News the testing it's doing looking for ways to reduce the danger of these fires in the cabin.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:09.380 line:1
The battery demos CBS saw burnt the cameras, and another test burnt a hole through the chair's seatback.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:12.080 line:1
The FAA giving us this guidance.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:22.120 line:1
If you notice that your device is overheating or getting hot to the touch, it is starting to swell or smoke, immediately call the flight attendant and they will fight that fire.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:28.020 line:1
And the FAA strictly requires that anything with a lithium ion battery go in your carry on luggage.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.340 line:1
That includes vapes and personal devices.

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:35.760 line:1
The FAA also telling us that a fire on a plane is the worst case scenario, Tony.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:36.340 line:1
Really is.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:37.440 line:1
Tom, thank you.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:47.260 line:1
And in Los Angeles today, the driver who crashed into that metro bus earlier this week arrived in court on a stretcher wrapped in blankets to face second degree murder charges.

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:51.040 line:1
Officials say she was intoxicated and driving against traffic.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:07:00.020 line:1
And to make things worse, a news helicopter covering the deadly accident also crashed, killing two on board and a pedestrian on the ground.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:01.840 line:1
Now to a CBS News exclusive.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:06.640 line:1
Our conversation with Patrick Clancy and his new wife, Dr. Rachel Dinis.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:19.260 line:1
60 Minutes correspondent Ross Douthat interviewed the couple about the case against Patrick's ex-wife, Lindsay Clancy, whose trial, of course, for the killing of their three small children ended earlier this month without a verdict.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:23.890 line:1
Patrick, for the first time since then, is taking questions on all of it.

00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:25.560 line:1
ROSS DOUTHAT: Are you angry at yourself?

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:29.200 line:1
Do you feel like you failed in some way in this period?

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:31.260 line:1
Or do you feel like you did the best you could?

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:36.300 line:1
I think I did the best I could with what I had at the time.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.040 line:1
And I live with the outcome.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:43.220 line:1
I live with it every day, but I think I did the best I could.

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:46.400 line:1
And joining me now is 60 Minutes correspondent Ross Douthat.

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:47.470 line:1
Ross, great to have you here.

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:48.390 line:1
Great to be here.

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:51.050 line:1
What can we expect from the piece on Sunday?

00:07:51.050 --> 00:08:11.910 line:1
I think the biggest thing is just a portrait of Patrick's life in the aftermath of this incredible tragedy, and what it's like to deal with that kind of grief, that kind of unimaginable experience as a father, and then what it means to try and rebuild your life in the shadow of that experience and try and go forward from there.

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:15.930 line:1
Why do you think Patrick Clancy wanted to do this interview?

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:26.290 line:1
I think from the beginning, he's been focused on trying to make sure that the memory of his children stays alive and stays close to the center of this story.

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:34.290 line:1
And obviously, there's been so many debates, so much controversy about all of the different issues around this story.

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:40.570 line:1
Mental health, marriage, men and women, the medical system, all of these things.

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:45.670 line:1
And there have been all kinds of, obviously, online accusations against Patrick himself.

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:55.950 line:1
And he felt his kids, Cora, Dawson, and Callan, were getting lost in all that noise and he wanted to put the spotlight back on them.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:57.590 line:1
And so it is.

00:08:57.590 --> 00:08:58.730 line:1
Ross, thank you very much.

00:08:58.730 --> 00:08:59.750 line:1
Thank you.

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:04.950 line:1
We'll have Ross Douthat's full exclusive report on 60 Minutes this Sunday.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:09.890 line:1
And tonight we're also hearing from the lone holdout juror in the Lindsay Clancy murder case.

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:16.250 line:1
The defense had argued that Clancy lost all control suffering from postpartum psychosis when she killed her three children.

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:20.170 line:1
The juror, however, says she knew exactly what she was doing.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:22.730 line:1
Our Shanelle Kaul reports.

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:27.710 line:1
SHANELLE KAUL: The holdout juror in the Lindsay Clancy murder trial has broken his silence.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:35.910 line:1
In a statement obtained by CBS News through his representative, Michael Desronvil says, I didn't have any doubts.

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:45.140 line:1
As I tried to explain different possible theories during deliberation, I kept getting cut off as if I had doubts based on the evidence present.

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:55.560 line:1
Based on all the physical evidence, key witnesses, and what the prosecution presents, I thought it was enough proof that she knew exactly what she was doing and planned.

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:58.200 line:1
He had said he had doubt.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:11.940 line:1
SHANELLE KAUL: Paula Devlin says she was one of the 11 jurors who wanted Lindsay Clancy to be found not criminally responsible, and told us she believes the holdout juror wasn't following the law when it came to reasonable doubt.

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:17.920 line:1
If he had shown us some evidence, then we all would have been OK with that.

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:20.100 line:1
But you don't feel like that's what happened here?

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:21.700 line:1
Unfortunately, no.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:23.620 line:1
Whose word matters more here?

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:27.220 line:1
The holdout juror or the 11 jurors.

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:28.080 line:1
The holdout juror.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:29.060 line:1
100%.

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:34.280 line:1
Otherwise, we could have a chilling effect on the process of jury deliberations.

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:43.810 line:1
The holding out really is a testament to what the jury deliberation process should look like.

00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:47.430 line:1
After the mistrial Desronvil was targeted online.

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:53.490 line:1
He hired a defamation lawyer, and the state, meantime, has not yet announced if it will retry Lindsay Clancy.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:53.990 line:1
Tony.

00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:55.770 line:1
Shanelle, thank you very much.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:11:03.230 line:1
And as we've been reporting, some of the most powerful people in the AI industry are filled with worry, even dread over what might go wrong.

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:07.950 line:1
But NVIDIA founder and CEO Jensen Huang is not one of those people.

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:15.490 line:1
He sat down with our Jo Ling Kent for an exclusive interview, arguing that now is not the time to slow down.

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:20.690 line:1
So should we slow down, and would that affect the race with China?

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:24.430 line:1
We should go as fast as we can, irrespective of anybody else.

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:37.170 line:1
We're going to go as fast as we can, but we would never, ever and never should ship products before they're ready, deliver products that are unsafe, and nobody's expecting us to do that.

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:43.230 line:1
But everybody's expecting us to succeed and help America be as prosperous as possible.

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:49.510 line:1
You can see more of Jo Ling Kent's exclusive report with NVIDIA CEO this weekend on CBS Sunday Morning.

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:56.170 line:1
Still ahead on the broadcast tonight, a mountain lion on the loose and a quite literally wild chase on the streets of San Francisco.

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:07.010 line:1
Just two blocks North of the San Francisco Zoo, a mountain lion from the wild this morning terrorized the neighborhood.

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:10.550 line:1
Carter Evans now with details.

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:16.250 line:1
CARTER EVANS: It's a wild police chase with a fugitive feline on the streets of San Francisco.

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:17.090 line:1
MAN: Coming our way.

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:23.210 line:1
JENSEN HUANG: But this mountain lion appears to have a problem with authority ignoring police containment lines.

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:26.880 line:1
I thought they were exaggerating when they said we needed to get out of the way before.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:28.410 line:1
But they were right.

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:31.150 line:1
[LAUGHS] It was just so fast.

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:32.170 line:1
MAN: It's beautiful.

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:37.930 line:1
CARTER EVANS: The animal was spotted this morning in a residential neighborhood and quickly became a media sensation.

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:44.300 line:1
REPORTER: Breaking news out of San Francisco, where a mountain lion has been spotted hiding under a bush.

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:45.860 line:1
CARTER EVANS: He's not lying.

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:46.880 line:1
There it is.

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:49.200 line:1
A terrifying sight for neighbors.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:51.940 line:1
My primal instincts kicked in and I ran.

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:54.280 line:1
CARTER EVANS: Even the mayor took to social media.

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:57.940 line:1
So just avoid the area, and we will keep you posted.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:01.660 line:1
CARTER EVANS: The chase finally ended with an unplanned cat nap.

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:06.000 line:1
Wildlife officers tranquilized the mountain lion and carried it to safety.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.040 line:1
MAN: There it is, guys.

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:11.700 line:1
CARTER EVANS: The cougar culprit in custody.

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:17.400 line:1
Now, you may have noticed authorities with their arms up, almost as if they were trying to catch the mountain lion.

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:22.040 line:1
And it may look absurd, but that's exactly what wildlife experts say you should do.

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:30.260 line:1
Hold up your arms, look big, and definitely do not turn and run, because that could trigger the lion's instinct to chase.

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:31.380 line:1
Tony.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:34.900 line:1
Carter, thank you very much.

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:41.270 line:1
When a police officer arrived at a major fire in Eau Claire, Wisconsin, early Sunday and heard that a young family was still inside.

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:43.190 line:1
He did not go looking for a firefighter.

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:50.530 line:1
He went right in himself, warned the family, and even scooped up a baby to help everyone get out and with no injuries.

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:54.270 line:1
Big cheers today for a police canine in Utah just released from the vet.

00:13:54.270 --> 00:14:01.470 line:1
This is Bruno, who broke both legs on the job investigating a suspicious package with the Park City Police Department.

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:05.970 line:1
But he is now on the mend and reunited with his family.

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:12.470 line:1
Up next on the road with Steve Hartman confronts the question that every parent eventually faces.

00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:13.910 line:1
Where did all the time go?

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:29.570 line:1
Finally tonight on the road where for a change, Steve Hartman stays home and for a reason every parent can relate to.

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:36.530 line:1
STEVE HARTMAN: It happened to my wife, Andrea, and me after our first child was born, and it happened constantly.

00:14:36.530 --> 00:14:40.250 line:1
Do you remember the advice that pretty much everybody gave us when we first had George?

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:41.500 line:1
Oh my gosh, it goes so fast.

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:42.670 line:1
STEVE HARTMAN: Cherish this.

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:43.410 line:1
It goes so fast.

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:43.910 line:1
Yeah.

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:46.910 line:1
And I could not imagine it going fast.

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:52.390 line:1
No, and now it feels like a blink of an eye.

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:56.910 line:1
STEVE HARTMAN: For us, that 18 year blink just happened.

00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:05.550 line:1
George is starting college, and although we have taught him everything he needs to know to live independently, except maybe how to pack a t-shirt.

00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:06.930 line:1
I suppose I'll figure that out.

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:10.710 line:1
STEVE HARTMAN: No one ever taught us how to deal with his vacancy.

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:15.790 line:1
How to get past this most puzzling parenting paradox.

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:17.570 line:1
He annoys us sometimes now.

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:19.510 line:1
Yeah, I think all children do.

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:20.990 line:1
We annoy him.

00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:22.830 line:1
We annoy him immensely.

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:23.970 line:1
[LAUGHS] Yeah.

00:15:23.970 --> 00:15:26.150 line:1
Why is it so hard then?

00:15:26.150 --> 00:15:32.270 line:1
You send your kids off to college, and you reduce in size in their rear view mirror.

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:33.980 line:1
STEVE HARTMAN: Yeah.

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:37.020 line:1
But they stay the same for you.

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:44.260 line:1
As parents, I think you just imagine your kids are always going to be there until one night there's an empty spot at the dinner table.

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:52.860 line:1
STEVE HARTMAN: In preparation for that moment, every couple of years I interviewed my kids, hoping to capture that time as it flew by.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:56.120 line:1
From those early days of steely confidence.

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:58.700 line:1
What do you want to be when you grow up?

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:01.260 line:1
Chemist or an astrophysicist.

00:16:01.260 --> 00:16:04.260 line:1
STEVE HARTMAN: Through the tender times of his first crush.

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:06.500 line:1
What makes her special?

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:13.700 line:1
Her liking me back, honestly, is what makes that really special.

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:18.620 line:1
STEVE HARTMAN: I even have those memorable years that hardly seem worth remembering.

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:21.980 line:1
They say that sometimes when kids hit puberty, they get moody.

00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:23.380 line:1
Whatever.

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:25.700 line:1
STEVE HARTMAN: I have saved it all.

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:31.280 line:1
Unfortunately, documenting the passage of time doesn't change our perception of it.

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:39.000 line:1
No matter how many photos you take or videos you record, it's still over in an instant.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:41.880 line:1
George left for school earlier this month.

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:43.680 line:1
He'll major in math.

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:47.200 line:1
And as for us remainders, I'm doing OK.

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:52.320 line:1
But my wife-- well, it's going to take her a little longer to adjust.

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:54.930 line:1
And not only because she misses him.

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:59.320 line:1
How much of it do you think is you're just worried about having to spend more time with me?

00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:00.620 line:1
Big part.

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:07.640 line:1
STEVE HARTMAN: Steve Hartman from an "emptier nest" in New York.

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:10.340 line:1
Steve Hartman on what he calls "the 18-year blink."

00:17:10.340 --> 00:17:11.680 line:1
Thank you, Steve.

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:13.220 line:1
That's another day in America.

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:14.300 line:1
I'm Tony Dokoupil.

00:17:14.300 --> 00:17:17.500 line:1
For all of us here at CBS News, thank you for watching this week.

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:19.640 line:1
We'll see you right back here on Monday.

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:20.140 line:1
Good night.
